						<statement>
							<p>
								False
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				For the demand function <m>D(p)=40-p</m>:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the elasticity of demand <m>E(p)</m> and evaluate it at <m>p=10.</m> Simplify your answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{gathered}
						E(p)=\frac{-p D'(p)}{D(p)}=\frac{-p(-1)}{40-p}=\frac{p}{40-p} \\
						E(10)=\frac{10}{40-10}=\frac{10}{30}=\frac{1}{3}
						\end{gathered}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Is the demand elastic or inelastic at <m>p=10</m>? To increase revenue, should you increase or decrease the price?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\frac{1}{3} \lt 1 \Rightarrow \text{ demand is inelastic at } p=10.
					</me>
					You should increase the price to increase revenue.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the price at which the demand is unit-elastic.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\text { want } \begin{aligned}
						E(p)=1 \amp =\frac{p}{40-p} \\
						40-p \amp =p \Rightarrow 40=2 p \\
						\amp p=20
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Suppose <m>f(x)</m> is a continuous function whose derivative is <m>f'(x)=x^{-1/3}(2-x)(x+4)^2</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task workspace="2in">
			<statement>
				<p>
					What are the critical numbers of <m>f(x)</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{array}{ll}
						x^{-1 / 3}: \amp x=0 \\
						2-x: \amp x=2 \\
						(x+4)^2: \amp x=-4
						\end{array}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					At what points, if any, does <m>f(x)</m> assume relative maximum and minimum values? Fully justify your answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Make sign chart.
					<me>
						\begin{array}{r:c|c|c|c} 
						\amp -5 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
						x^{-1 / 3} \amp - \amp - \amp + \amp + \\
						2-x \amp + \amp + \amp + \amp - \\
						(x+4)^2 \amp + \amp + \amp + \amp + \\
						\hline f' \amp - \amp - \amp + \amp - \\
						\hline f \amp \searrow \amp \searrow \amp \nearrow \amp \searrow
						\end{array}
					</me>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\amp x=-4 \text { not a rel. max nor rel. min } \\
						\amp x=0 \text { is a rel. min. } \\
						\amp x=2 \text { is a rel. max. }
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				On the axes below, draw the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> which is continuous everywhere except <m>x=1</m> that satisfies the following conditions:
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>f(0)=1</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>f(x)</m> has a vertical asymptote at <m>x=1</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>f'(x)&lt;0</m> on <m>(4,\infty)</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>f'(x)&gt;0</m> on <m>(-\infty, 1)\cup(1,4)</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>f''(x)&gt;0</m> on <m>(-\infty, 1)</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>f''(x)&lt;0</m> on <m>( 1,\infty)</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				 
			</p>
		</introduction>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				 A rectangular sheep pen is to be enclosed by a wooden fence and divided into two equal parts by a chain link fence parallel to one of the sides. The wooden fencing will cost <m>\$4</m> per foot, while the chain link will cost <m>\$ 2</m> per foot. With <m>\$400</m> to spend, what dimensions for the outer rectangle will create the largest total area? Justify your solution is optimal.
			</p>
			<p>
				
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{gathered}
					A=x y=\left(40-\frac{8}{10} x\right) x \\
					\text { cost: } C= \underbrace{2(4 x)+2(4 y)+2 y=400}_{=8 x+10 y=400 \Rightarrow y=\frac{400-8 x}{10}} \Rightarrow y=40-\frac{4}{5} x
					\end{gathered}
				</me>
				<me>
					\begin{gathered}
					A=\left(40-\frac{4}{5} x\right) x=40 x-\frac{4}{5} x^2 \\
					A'=40-\frac{8}{5} x=0 \\
					x=40 \cdot \frac{5}{8}=25 \\
					y=40-\frac{4}{5} \cdot 25=40-20 \\
					y=20
					\end{gathered}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				For <m>f(x)=x^4-4x^3+10</m>:
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the intervals of increasing/decreasing. Give your answers using interval notation. Show all work.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						f'(x)=4 x^3-12 x^2=4 x^2(x-3) \quad \text{CN}: x=0,3
					</me>
					<me>
						\begin{tabular}{cc|c|c} 
						\amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\
						4 x^2 \amp + \amp + \amp + \\
						x-3 \amp - \amp - \amp + \\
						\hline f' \amp - \amp - \amp + \\
						\hline f \amp \searrow \amp \searrow \amp \nearrow
						\end{tabular}
					</me>
					Dec: <m>(-\infty, 0) \cup(0,3)</m>
					Inc: <m>(3, \infty)</m>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the intervals of concave up/concave down. Give your answers using interval notation. Show all work.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						f''(x)=12 x^2-24 x=12 x(x-2)=0 \text { at } x=0,2
					</me>
					<me>
						\begin{array}{cc|c|c} 
						\amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
						12 x \amp - \amp + \amp + \\
						x-2 \amp - \amp - \amp + \\
						\hline f'' \amp + \amp - \amp + \\
						\hline f \amp \cup \amp \cap \amp \cup
						\end{array}
					</me>
					Concave up: <m>(-\infty, 0) \cup(2, \infty)</m>
					Concave down: <m>(0,2)</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				A bus company will rent a bus that holds 50 people to groups of 30 people or more. If a group contains exactly 30 people, each person pays <m>\$80</m>. In large groups, everybody’s fare is reduced by <m>\$2</m> for each person in excess of 30. Determine the size of the group for which the bus company’s revenue will be greatest. Justify why your solution is optimal.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				let <m>x=</m> number of people in excess of 30.
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\text { Rev }=R(x) \amp =(x+30)(80-2 x) \quad 0 \leq x \leq 20
					\amp =80 x-2 x^2+2400-60 x \\
					\amp =-2 x^2+20 x+2400
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					R'(x)=-4 x \amp +20=0 \\
					x=\frac{20}{4} \amp =5 \text { max b/c } R''=-4 \lt 0.
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				max revenue occurs when the group size is <m>35=30+5</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find the absolute maximum and absolute minimum of <m>g(x)=6x^2-x^3-6</m> on <m>[-1,3]</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					g'(x) \amp =12 x-3 x^2=3 x(4-x) \\
					\amp \quad \text { C.N. } x=0,4\\
					g(-1) \amp =6(-1)^2-(-1)^3-6=1 \\
					g(0) \amp =-6 \leftarrow \text { absolute min } \\
					g(3) \amp =6 \cdot 3^2-3^3-6 \\
					\amp =54-27-6=21 \leftarrow \text { absolute max. }
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find <m>f'(x)</m> if <m>f(x) = \ln(x^2+2x)</m>. You do not need to simplify your final answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						f'(x)=\frac{1}{x^2+2 x}(2 x+2)=\frac{2 x+2}{x^2+2 x}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find <m>g'(x)</m> if <m>g(x)=e^{x^2+4x}</m>. You do not need to simplify your final answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						g'(x)=e^{x^2+4 x}(2 x+4)
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
